<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Larova · German strings.

  A translation, not a source: never fix wording here. Fix the English in values/strings.xml and
  re-translate, or the two drift apart and nobody can tell which one is right. See
  docs/localization.md.

  Two decisions run through the whole file and are worth knowing before changing one line of it:

  · The address form is avoided wherever German allows it — infinitives ("PIN eingeben") and
    impersonal constructions rather than "du" or "Sie". That matches the English, which almost
    never addresses the reader either, and it sidesteps a choice that would otherwise have to be
    right for a grandmother, a babysitter and a parent at once. Where an address form is
    unavoidable, it is "Sie": the person reading a caregiver screen is often not family.

  · German runs about 30 % longer than English, so the wording is kept as short as the meaning
    allows. Anything on a button, in a chip or on a tile is shorter here than a literal
    translation would be.

  Three groups still want a native read-through before release, per docs/localization.md §5: the
  help bar and contact sheet, the two view names, and the templates.
-->
